"Rich Holoch" <holoch@ccnet.com> wrote:

>I am an Oracle DBA who is inheriting two large DEC UNIX systems where the
>previous UNIX Systems Administrator made some big mistakes setting up the
>disk subsystem.  There is a nasty mixture of ufs,  lsm and advfs used
>across the system,  and I believe the advfs GUI is partly to blame.  For
>example,  the /usr file system has LSM and ADVFS volumes.  After a hardware
>crash,  /usr now has a corrupted advfs file system,  and recovering from
>this has been one horrible nightmare.  I'll probably have to recover by
>re-installing UNIX and then recovering selectively from my / and /usr tape
>backup.

>My question is - to advfs or not to advfs.  Also - should I keep it simple
>and set everything up with LSM?  The GUI built into advfs is NOT a
>motivator for me,  especially seeing how it will let poorly trained UNIX
>Systems Administrators mess up their disk subsystem.

I use advfs and avoid LSM.  The avoidance of LSM is a site bias as
somthing went bad here with long before I arrived and it hasn't been
trusted since.  But then again one of our departments has had a bad
experience with advfs and doesn't touch it now and only uses ufs.

I never use the GUI for advfs.  I use the command line commands, there
an't that many and they are well documented in man.   The best
starting point is man advfs.

As yet I have not had a major problem with advfs and have found it
very useful in managing disk space and has been more robust in system
crashes than the few ufs file systems that I have.  The only catch
that I have had is that an advfs in its standard format can only
handle 200000 to 250000 files how ever there is undocumented flag in
domain creation (-x that allows you to up the number).  We have one
advfs with over 800000 files (web cache).

Greg






